<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre de Coubertin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles Pierre de Frédy, Baron de Coubertin, Baron de Coubertin, Pierre de Frédy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre de Coubertin was a French educator and historian, co-founder of the International Olympic Committee (IOC) and its second president, known as the father of the modern Olympic Games. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1863 into an aristocratic family, he studied law and public affairs at Sciences Po and was particularly active in promoting sport in French schools. A congress he organised at the Sorbonne in Paris on 23 June 1894 set the first modern Olympic Games for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> in 1896 and the second for Paris in 1900. He became president of the IOC in 1896 and was succeeded in 1925 by Henri de Baillet-Latour. He died of a heart attack in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1937 and was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-de-coubertin.jpg" alt="Pierre de Coubertin" width="330" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author (but see here) · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pierre_de_Coubertin_Anefo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Revived by <b>Pierre de Coubertin</b>, the Games drew some 240 athletes from 14 nations.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-6">6 April in history</a></li></ul></section>
